# StayAssist V1 — Operational Flow & Database Architecture Guide
> **Generated:** 2026-04-09 | **Status:** Production-Ready Flow (Zero Seed Scripts)

This document outlines the actual end-to-end operational flow of the StayAssist V1 platform, based purely on the defined database schema, foreign-key relationships, and application code.

**This is how the system operates in a clean, production environment without any mock seed data.**

---

## 🏗️ 1. ID Generation Architecture

In StayAssist V1, you **never** manually define IDs like `"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000"`.
Every entity across the schema uses **PostgreSQL's `gen_random_uuid()`** as the primary key default.

When you run an `INSERT` statement without an `id` field, PostgreSQL automatically generates a v4 cryptographically secure UUID and assigns it.

---

## 🏥 2. The Initialization Flow (Onboarding a Facility)

Because of strict foreign key constraints, you must build the data hierarchy from the top down. A hospital cannot exist without an organization, a department cannot exist without a hospital, and a user cannot exist without a department.

This is a **one-time manual administration task** per health system.

### Step 2.1: Create the Organization
*The highest-level container (e.g., "Apollo Hospitals Enterprise")*

```sql
INSERT INTO public.organizations (name)
VALUES ('City General Hospital Group')
RETURNING id;
-- 📌 COPY THIS UUID (We will call it <ORG_ID>)
```

### Step 2.2: Create the Hospital
*A physical facility belonging to the organization.*

```sql
INSERT INTO public.hospitals (organization_id, name, nabh_accredited, jci_accredited, address)
VALUES (
  '<ORG_ID>',
  'City General Hospital Main Campus',
  TRUE, FALSE,
  '123 Main Road, Mumbai - 400001'
)
RETURNING id;
-- 📌 COPY THIS UUID (We will call it <HOSPITAL_ID>)
```

### Step 2.3: Create SLA Configurations
*Define the compliance thresholds for the new hospital based on NABH/JCI standards.*

```sql
INSERT INTO public.sla_configurations (hospital_id, severity_level, max_acknowledgement_hours, max_resolution_hours)
VALUES
  ('<HOSPITAL_ID>', 'critical', 1, 24),
  ('<HOSPITAL_ID>', 'high',     4, 72),
  ('<HOSPITAL_ID>', 'medium',   8, 168),
  ('<HOSPITAL_ID>', 'low',      24, 720);
```
*(Note: Code must be updated in Sprint C.3 to read these dynamically instead of using the hardcoded 10-minute mock override).*

### Step 2.4: Create Departments
*The routing destinations for complaints.*

```sql
INSERT INTO public.departments (hospital_id, name, escalation_level)
VALUES
  ('<HOSPITAL_ID>', 'Quality & Patient Safety',  3),
  ('<HOSPITAL_ID>', 'Emergency & Trauma',        1),
  ('<HOSPITAL_ID>', 'Administration',            4)
RETURNING id, name;
-- 📌 COPY THESE UUIDs (We will call them <DEPT_ID>)
```

